docs/current-state.md:5:## What exists
docs/current-state.md:6:
docs/current-state.md:7:The repository builds one SwiftPM macOS 14 executable, `ReluxProxy`, packaged by
docs/current-state.md:8:shell scripts as a menu bar `.app` and DMG.
docs/current-state.md:9:
docs/current-state.md:10:The app:
docs/current-state.md:11:
docs/current-state.md:12:- stores an ordered collection of profiles as one schema-versioned JSON Data
docs/current-state.md:13:  value under `legacySocksProfiles.v1`, with stable UUID identity and unique
docs/current-state.md:14:  explicit local ports;
docs/current-state.md:15:- migrates the legacy `sshHost`, `sshAccount`, and `localPort` values exactly
docs/current-state.md:16:  once, leaves those keys untouched for rollback, and never resurrects deleted
docs/current-state.md:17:  profiles after the migration marker is set;
docs/current-state.md:18:- owns one independent runtime session per profile, allowing multiple SSH
docs/current-state.md:19:  children and SOCKS tunnels to connect concurrently on different local ports;
docs/current-state.md:20:- launches `/usr/bin/ssh -N -C -D 127.0.0.1:<port>` with fail-fast, keepalive,
docs/current-state.md:21:  timeout, batch-mode, and quiet logging options;
docs/current-state.md:22:- passes an empty account as the bare host target, allowing system OpenSSH to
docs/current-state.md:23:  use a matching `User` setting (or its normal default); a non-empty account is
docs/current-state.md:24:  sent with `-l account` while the destination remains the bare host or alias,
docs/current-state.md:25:  which explicitly overrides only the SSH user;
docs/current-state.md:26:- accepts an IP address, hostname, or `Host` alias and lets the system client
docs/current-state.md:27:  apply matching `~/.ssh/config` behavior such as `HostName`, `Port`,
docs/current-state.md:28:  `IdentityFile`, and `ProxyJump`;
docs/current-state.md:29:- binds the SOCKS listener only to IPv4 loopback (`127.0.0.1`) and keeps a
docs/current-state.md:30:  separate trailing 4,000-character in-memory diagnostic for every session;
docs/current-state.md:31:- rejects duplicate saved ports and externally occupied loopback ports without
docs/current-state.md:32:  stopping or modifying another session or process;
docs/current-state.md:33:- reports connecting, connected, disconnecting, disconnected, and failed states
docs/current-state.md:34:  per row, with aggregate menu-bar status;
docs/current-state.md:35:- prevents profile edits and removal while that profile owns a starting,
docs/current-state.md:36:  connected, or stopping child;
docs/current-state.md:37:- stops every child it owns on normal quit and starts every saved profile
docs/current-state.md:38:  disconnected on the next launch, with no automatic retry or reconnect;
docs/current-state.md:39:- exposes every listed endpoint as an Application SOCKS5 proxy — TCP only; not
docs/current-state.md:40:  a system VPN or system-wide routing. Users configure a SOCKS-aware client
docs/current-state.md:41:  manually and enable proxy DNS/SOCKS5h when private names need the remote
docs/current-state.md:42:  resolver;
docs/current-state.md:43:- intentionally runs outside the App Sandbox so it can spawn system SSH.
docs/current-state.md:44:
docs/current-state.md:45:Tests cover profile migration and persistence, command construction and account
docs/current-state.md:46:precedence, duplicate and OS port conflicts, concurrent independent lifecycle,
docs/current-state.md:47:stale callback protection, diagnostics, active mutation rejection, and
docs/current-state.md:48:coordinated shutdown. CI runs `swift test` and builds an ad-hoc-signed universal
docs/current-state.md:49:application bundle.
docs/current-state.md:50:
docs/current-state.md:51:The v0.2.0 release automation imports the Relux Works Developer ID certificate
docs/current-state.md:52:into a disposable keychain, builds a universal app, signs the DMG, notarizes and
docs/current-state.md:53:staples it, validates it with Gatekeeper, publishes checksums, and verifies both
docs/current-state.md:54:the versioned and stable `ReluxProxy.dmg` GitLab Release assets. It refuses the
docs/current-state.md:55:wrong host, project, accepted commit, runner, identity, or unsigned fallback.
docs/current-state.md:56:Production publication remains externally blocked until the exact Relux GitLab
docs/current-state.md:57:project, authorization, protected macOS runner, and notarization variables are
docs/current-state.md:58:confirmed; no GitLab v0.2.0 release is currently claimed.
docs/current-state.md:59:
docs/current-state.md:60:## What does not exist
docs/current-state.md:61:
docs/current-state.md:62:The current app is not a system VPN and does not contain:
docs/current-state.md:63:
docs/current-state.md:64:- iOS targets;
docs/current-state.md:65:- `NETunnelProviderManager` or packet-tunnel extensions;
docs/current-state.md:66:- a userspace TCP/IP stack or packetFlow bridge;
docs/current-state.md:67:- an embedded SSH implementation;
docs/current-state.md:68:- UDP or system DNS forwarding;
docs/current-state.md:69:- the remote `relux-relay` executable/protocol;
docs/current-state.md:70:- shared profile/Keychain state between host and extension;
docs/current-state.md:71:- Network Extension entitlements or provisioning;
docs/current-state.md:72:- TestFlight/App Store release automation.
docs/current-state.md:73:
docs/current-state.md:74:## Implemented legacy migration
docs/current-state.md:75:
docs/current-state.md:76:The [multi-profile usage guide](../README.md#use) describes the user-facing
docs/current-state.md:77:workflow. The implementation above provides the one-time migration, profile
docs/current-state.md:78:persistence, OpenSSH account precedence, port ownership, diagnostics, and
docs/current-state.md:79:independent lifecycle. Runtime state and reconnect intent are never persisted.
docs/current-state.md:80:
docs/current-state.md:81:The [remote VPN reachability guide](socks-vpn-reachability.md) documents the
docs/current-state.md:82:same application-level boundary, proxy-side DNS, and redacted diagnostics. A
docs/current-state.md:83:remote VPN route, the authenticated `sshd` forwarding process/account policy,
docs/current-state.md:84:and the remote firewall must all permit the requested TCP destination; Relux
docs/current-state.md:85:Proxy does not change local system proxy, DNS, or routes.
docs/current-state.md:86:
docs/current-state.md:87:The planned VPN product in `.spec/` remains a new architecture, not an
docs/current-state.md:88:incremental toggle inside the legacy SOCKS controller.
docs/current-state.md:89:
docs/current-state.md:90:For future VPN work:
docs/current-state.md:91:
docs/current-state.md:92:- the stable macOS release path must remain reproducible;
docs/current-state.md:93:- any migration from legacy SOCKS profiles to VPN profiles still needs its own
docs/current-state.md:94:  explicit product decision;
docs/current-state.md:95:- the system-SSH implementation may remain as a macOS legacy mode, be replaced,
docs/current-state.md:96:  or be retired only through a product decision;
docs/current-state.md:97:- SwiftPM tests and release history must not be silently discarded;
docs/current-state.md:98:- Network Extension code must not be backported into the current manual app
docs/current-state.md:99:  without the architecture and entitlement gates in `.spec/validation.md`.
.spec/platform-distribution.md:1:# Platform and distribution specification
.spec/platform-distribution.md:2:
.spec/platform-distribution.md:3:## Project shape
.spec/platform-distribution.md:4:
.spec/platform-distribution.md:5:The current SwiftPM executable is insufficient for embedded Network Extension
.spec/platform-distribution.md:6:targets and iOS distribution. The target project is a generated Xcode workspace,
.spec/platform-distribution.md:7:using the Relux Works Tuist convention unless the project migration task records
.spec/platform-distribution.md:8:a better supported choice.
.spec/platform-distribution.md:9:
.spec/platform-distribution.md:10:Planned targets:
.spec/platform-distribution.md:11:
.spec/platform-distribution.md:12:- `ReluxProxyMac` containing app;
.spec/platform-distribution.md:13:- `ReluxProxyMacTunnel` packet tunnel extension;
.spec/platform-distribution.md:14:- `ReluxProxyIOS` containing app;
.spec/platform-distribution.md:15:- `ReluxProxyIOSTunnel` packet tunnel extension;
.spec/platform-distribution.md:16:- `ReluxTunnelCore` Swift package shared by both providers;
.spec/platform-distribution.md:17:- `ReluxTunnelHarness` macOS CLI for fast packet/SSH/relay experiments;
.spec/platform-distribution.md:18:- `relux-relay` portable remote executable and protocol test target.
.spec/platform-distribution.md:19:
.spec/platform-distribution.md:20:The existing menu bar SOCKS app remains buildable until an explicit migration
.spec/platform-distribution.md:21:task replaces it. Migration MUST preserve or deliberately retire its profile
.spec/platform-distribution.md:22:defaults and release history.
.spec/platform-distribution.md:23:
.spec/platform-distribution.md:24:## Apple capabilities
.spec/platform-distribution.md:25:
.spec/platform-distribution.md:26:Both containing apps manage custom VPN configurations through
.spec/platform-distribution.md:27:`NETunnelProviderManager`. Host and extension App IDs require the Network
.spec/platform-distribution.md:28:Extension packet-tunnel entitlement and matching provisioning profiles. App
.spec/platform-distribution.md:29:Groups and Keychain Sharing provide the minimum app/extension state boundary.
.spec/platform-distribution.md:30:
.spec/platform-distribution.md:31:**Gate P0:** confirm Relux Works organization enrollment, bundle IDs, Network
.spec/platform-distribution.md:32:Extension capability/profiles, and device installation before packet-plane work
.spec/platform-distribution.md:33:is considered viable.
.spec/platform-distribution.md:34:
.spec/platform-distribution.md:35:The app must be primarily a networking product and accurately disclose its
.spec/platform-distribution.md:36:extension and VPN behavior. Apple App Review Guideline 5.4 requires VPN apps to
.spec/platform-distribution.md:37:be offered by an organization and imposes privacy and regional-law obligations.
.spec/platform-distribution.md:38:
.spec/platform-distribution.md:39:## Minimum platform policy
.spec/platform-distribution.md:40:
.spec/platform-distribution.md:41:Exact deployment targets are selected during project migration from current
.spec/platform-distribution.md:42:API/support evidence. The baseline MUST support:
.spec/platform-distribution.md:43:
.spec/platform-distribution.md:44:- a current iOS release on a physical iPhone used for memory/lifecycle gates;
.spec/platform-distribution.md:45:- a current macOS release on Apple silicon;
.spec/platform-distribution.md:46:- the oldest iOS/macOS versions for which all selected Network Extension,
.spec/platform-distribution.md:47:  Network.framework, Swift concurrency, signing, and dependency requirements can
.spec/platform-distribution.md:48:  be tested in CI.
.spec/platform-distribution.md:49:
.spec/platform-distribution.md:50:No deployment target is lowered by adding private API or fragile compatibility
.spec/platform-distribution.md:51:shims.
.spec/platform-distribution.md:52:
.spec/platform-distribution.md:53:## Signing and release channels
.spec/platform-distribution.md:54:
.spec/platform-distribution.md:55:### macOS
.spec/platform-distribution.md:56:
.spec/platform-distribution.md:57:Developer ID distribution uses hardened runtime, nested signing in inside-out
.spec/platform-distribution.md:58:order, notarization, stapling, Gatekeeper validation, checksums, and provenance
.spec/platform-distribution.md:59:attestation. The packet tunnel extension and containing app need compatible
.spec/platform-distribution.md:60:entitlements/profiles. A stable `ReluxProxy.dmg` release asset may continue, but
.spec/platform-distribution.md:61:on a private repository it requires authenticated GitHub access.
.spec/platform-distribution.md:62:
.spec/platform-distribution.md:63:### iOS
.spec/platform-distribution.md:64:
.spec/platform-distribution.md:65:iOS uses Apple Development for device spikes and Apple Distribution through
.spec/platform-distribution.md:66:TestFlight/App Store. Developer ID and notarization do not apply to iOS.
.spec/platform-distribution.md:67:TestFlight is required for lifecycle, entitlement, and review-preparation
.spec/platform-distribution.md:68:iterations before App Store submission.
.spec/platform-distribution.md:69:
.spec/platform-distribution.md:70:### macOS self-update
.spec/platform-distribution.md:71:
.spec/platform-distribution.md:72:The macOS app ships an in-app self-update channel so security fixes reach users
.spec/platform-distribution.md:73:without a manual re-download (ADR-018). Constraints:
.spec/platform-distribution.md:74:
.spec/platform-distribution.md:75:- Sparkle 2.x with an EdDSA (ed25519) signed appcast; the public key is pinned in
.spec/platform-distribution.md:76:  `Info.plist` (`SUPublicEDKey`), the private key is a CI secret held in the same
.spec/platform-distribution.md:77:  custody as the Developer ID / notarization credentials.
.spec/platform-distribution.md:78:- Every update payload is an already-notarized Developer ID build, so hardened
.spec/platform-distribution.md:79:  runtime, Gatekeeper, and stapling guarantees are preserved end to end. The
.spec/platform-distribution.md:80:  updater embeds without introducing notarization-breaking entitlements.
.spec/platform-distribution.md:81:- The appcast and update assets are published from the same authenticated GitHub
.spec/platform-distribution.md:82:  release channel as the notarized DMG; a missing or invalid signature fails
.spec/platform-distribution.md:83:  closed, downgrades are refused outside an explicit rollback channel, and a
.spec/platform-distribution.md:84:  payload whose team identifier does not match is rejected.
.spec/platform-distribution.md:85:- iOS is out of scope for self-update: the App Store owns iOS updates.
.spec/platform-distribution.md:86:- Key custody, rotation, appcast withdrawal, emergency stop, and rollback are
.spec/platform-distribution.md:87:  documented in the self-update runbook and referenced by the threat model
.spec/platform-distribution.md:88:  (supply-chain adversary, §4.E).
.spec/platform-distribution.md:89:
.spec/platform-distribution.md:90:## CI/CD contract
.spec/platform-distribution.md:91:
.spec/platform-distribution.md:92:Pull requests run documentation/board validation plus builds and tests available
.spec/platform-distribution.md:93:without production credentials. Release workflows use GitHub Environments and
.spec/platform-distribution.md:94:least-privilege secrets; no certificate, private key, issuer credential, or
.spec/platform-distribution.md:95:provisioning secret is committed.
.spec/platform-distribution.md:96:
.spec/platform-distribution.md:97:Required pipelines:
.spec/platform-distribution.md:98:
.spec/platform-distribution.md:99:1. shared-core unit and protocol conformance tests on macOS;
.spec/platform-distribution.md:100:2. macOS host/extension build, entitlement inspection, signing verification,
.spec/platform-distribution.md:101:   notarization, DMG publication, checksums, and stable-name asset;
.spec/platform-distribution.md:102:3. iOS host/extension archive, entitlement/provisioning inspection, and
.spec/platform-distribution.md:103:   TestFlight upload;
.spec/platform-distribution.md:104:4. reproducible relay cross-build matrix, tests, checksums, manifest generation,
.spec/platform-distribution.md:105:   and bundling into Apple targets;
.spec/platform-distribution.md:106:5. dependency license/SBOM and secret scanning;
.spec/platform-distribution.md:107:6. semantic version/tag validation and release notes.
.spec/platform-distribution.md:108:
.spec/platform-distribution.md:109:Production release jobs are serialized. Build artifacts retain exact source
.spec/platform-distribution.md:110:commit, dependency lockfiles, relay manifest, protocol version, bundle versions,
.spec/platform-distribution.md:111:and signing identity metadata.
.spec/platform-distribution.md:112:
.spec/platform-distribution.md:113:## App Review package
.spec/platform-distribution.md:114:
.spec/platform-distribution.md:115:The submission package includes:
.spec/platform-distribution.md:116:
.spec/platform-distribution.md:117:- an accurate VPN data-flow explanation and test SSH host instructions;
.spec/platform-distribution.md:118:- in-app VPN data disclosure and public privacy policy;
.spec/platform-distribution.md:119:- Network Extension entitlement purpose;
.spec/platform-distribution.md:120:- regional licensing notes or excluded storefronts;
.spec/platform-distribution.md:121:- explanation that users provide their own SSH exit host;
.spec/platform-distribution.md:122:- degraded-mode behavior and system-excluded traffic disclosure;
.spec/platform-distribution.md:123:- review account/profile or reproducible setup that does not expose private
.spec/platform-distribution.md:124:  production credentials.
README.md:1:# Relux Proxy
README.md:2:
README.md:3:Relux Proxy is a native macOS menu bar app for saved, independently controlled
README.md:4:SSH dynamic SOCKS5 tunnels. Multiple profiles can run concurrently when they
README.md:5:use different local ports. Every tunnel uses the system `/usr/bin/ssh`, so
README.md:6:hosts, keys, jump hosts, and other options from `~/.ssh/config` continue to
README.md:7:work; Relux Proxy does not parse or rewrite that file.
README.md:8:
README.md:9:The repository also contains the approved specifications and implementation
README.md:10:backlog for a future macOS/iOS system VPN over SSH. That VPN is not implemented
README.md:11:yet. Start with [the specification index](.spec/README.md),
README.md:12:[the current-state inventory](docs/current-state.md), and
README.md:13:[the saved task-board workflow](docs/project-management.md).
README.md:14:
README.md:15:## Use
README.md:16:
README.md:17:1. Open Relux Proxy from Applications. On the first launch after upgrading, the
README.md:18:   previous `sshHost`, `sshAccount`, and `localPort` settings migrate into one
README.md:19:   saved profile. A clean install starts with `administrator@relux` on
README.md:20:   `127.0.0.1:1080`.
README.md:21:2. Use **Add profile** to save another SSH-reachable IP address, hostname, or
README.md:22:   `~/.ssh/config` alias and an explicit local SOCKS port. Ports must be unique
README.md:23:   across saved profiles.
README.md:24:3. Leave **Account** empty to let system OpenSSH use a matching config's `User`
README.md:25:   or its normal login-user default. A non-empty account is passed as an
README.md:26:   explicit `-l` override without changing the alias or SSH configuration.
README.md:27:4. Choose **Connect** on each profile you want to run. Profiles with different
README.md:28:   ports connect independently; a failure or occupied port affects only that
README.md:29:   row.
README.md:30:5. In Firefox, open **Settings → Network Settings**, select **Manual proxy
README.md:31:   configuration**, and enter the endpoint shown on the chosen profile:
README.md:32:
README.md:33:   - SOCKS Host: `127.0.0.1`
README.md:34:   - Port: the profile's saved port
README.md:35:   - SOCKS v5
README.md:36:   - Proxy DNS when using SOCKS v5
README.md:37:
README.md:38:6. Disconnect a profile before editing or removing it. Quitting Relux Proxy
README.md:39:   stops every SSH process launched by the current app run. Profiles remain
README.md:40:   saved, but tunnels do not reconnect automatically on the next launch.
README.md:41:
README.md:42:### Installed-Mac two-tunnel check
README.md:43:
README.md:44:After copying `ReluxProxy.app` to Applications, create two profiles with
README.md:45:different ports and connect both. Configure each SOCKS-aware client (or client
README.md:46:profile) with `127.0.0.1` and that profile's saved port. On the installed Mac,
README.md:47:use an organization-owned or otherwise authorized HTTPS endpoint that reports
README.md:48:the request's exit identity:
README.md:49:
README.md:50:```bash
README.md:51:FIRST_SOCKS_PORT=1080
README.md:52:SECOND_SOCKS_PORT=1081
README.md:53:EXIT_IDENTITY_URL='https://<AUTHORIZED_EXIT_IDENTITY_ENDPOINT>'
README.md:54:
README.md:55:/usr/bin/curl --fail --silent --show-error \
README.md:56:  --connect-timeout 10 --max-time 20 \
README.md:57:  --socks5-hostname "127.0.0.1:$FIRST_SOCKS_PORT" \
README.md:58:  "$EXIT_IDENTITY_URL"
README.md:59:
README.md:60:/usr/bin/curl --fail --silent --show-error \
README.md:61:  --connect-timeout 10 --max-time 20 \
README.md:62:  --socks5-hostname "127.0.0.1:$SECOND_SOCKS_PORT" \
README.md:63:  "$EXIT_IDENTITY_URL"
README.md:64:```
README.md:65:
README.md:66:Compare each result with the expected exit identity for its SSH host. The
README.md:67:values may legitimately match when both hosts share an egress, but each command
README.md:68:must succeed through its selected local port. Then disconnect one profile,
README.md:69:repeat the request through the other port, and confirm that the remaining
README.md:70:tunnel and its exit identity are unchanged. This physical external-SSH-host
README.md:71:step is intentionally not run by the automated test suite.
README.md:72:
README.md:73:## Remote VPN reachability
README.md:74:
README.md:75:Each listed endpoint is an Application SOCKS5 proxy — TCP only; not a system VPN
README.md:76:or system-wide routing. Configure an individual SOCKS-aware application to use
README.md:77:the endpoint. For private hostnames, enable the application's proxy DNS, remote
README.md:78:DNS, or SOCKS5h option so the remote SSH machine resolves the name instead of
README.md:79:the source Mac.
README.md:80:
README.md:81:The remote VPN must route the destination and permit sockets opened by the
README.md:82:authenticated `sshd` forwarding process/account; SSH forwarding policy and the
README.md:83:remote firewall must also allow the connection. SOCKS-unaware applications,
README.md:84:UDP, ICMP, and QUIC-only traffic do not traverse this dynamic forward. Relux
README.md:85:Proxy does not alter macOS system proxy, DNS, or route settings.
README.md:86:
README.md:87:Use the [redacted private-network reachability guide](docs/socks-vpn-reachability.md)
README.md:88:for numeric-IP, proxy-DNS hostname, and application-level checks.
README.md:89:
README.md:90:## Download
README.md:91:
README.md:92:The latest signed and notarized build always has the same asset name and URL
README.md:93:(GitHub authentication is required while this repository is private):
README.md:94:
README.md:95:https://github.com/relux-works/relux-proxy/releases/latest/download/ReluxProxy.dmg
README.md:96:
README.md:97:Versioned DMGs and SHA-256 checksums are available on the GitHub Releases page.
README.md:98:The v0.2.0 GitLab release is intentionally not advertised as published until
README.md:99:the Relux-owned GitLab hostname and project are confirmed and its protected
README.md:100:release pipeline passes. See the
README.md:101:[GitLab release runbook](docs/gitlab-release.md) for the exact gate and current
README.md:102:external blocker.
README.md:103:
README.md:104:## Development
README.md:105:
README.md:106:Requirements: macOS 14 or newer and Xcode 16 or newer.
README.md:107:
README.md:108:```bash
README.md:109:make lint
README.md:110:make test
README.md:111:make app
README.md:112:make run
README.md:113:```
README.md:114:
README.md:115:### Development tools
README.md:116:
README.md:117:| Command | Purpose | Output or effect |
README.md:118:| --- | --- | --- |
README.md:119:| `make lint` | Run strict `swift-format` checks over app and test sources. | Diagnostics only; source files are not modified. |
README.md:120:| `make test` | Run the SwiftPM test suite. | SwiftPM build and test data in `.build/`. |
README.md:121:| `make app` | Build and sign the macOS application. | `dist/ReluxProxy.app`. |
README.md:122:| `make dmg` | Build the app, then package a disk image. | `dist/ReluxProxy-v<version>-universal.dmg`. |
README.md:123:| `make run` | Build the app, then open it. | Opens `dist/ReluxProxy.app`. |
README.md:124:| `shellcheck scripts/*.sh` | Lint packaging and release shell automation. | Diagnostics only; scripts are not modified. |
README.md:125:| GitLab `validate` pipeline | Run shell lint, Swift tests/build, and an ad-hoc universal app build on a macOS runner. | GitLab job logs and development build artifacts. |
README.md:126:| GitLab protected `v0.2.0` pipeline | Build, Developer ID sign, notarize, staple, Gatekeeper-check, publish, and re-download/verify the release assets. | GitLab Package Registry, Release assets, and maintainer-only audit artifacts. |
README.md:127:
README.md:128:The app is intentionally not sandboxed because it needs to launch the system
README.md:129:SSH executable. Release bundles use the hardened runtime and are signed with
README.md:130:the Relux Works Developer ID. The implemented legacy SOCKS behavior is
README.md:131:documented in the [current-state inventory](docs/current-state.md); the
README.md:132:[specification index](.spec/README.md) remains limited to the separate planned
README.md:133:VPN.
README.md:134:
README.md:135:Contributors must follow [CONTRIBUTING.md](CONTRIBUTING.md). Planned VPN work is
README.md:136:tracked in `.task-board/`; no VPN implementation should begin without explicit
README.md:137:approval of the generated plan.
README.md:138:
README.md:139:## Release
README.md:140:
README.md:141:Relux Proxy v0.2.0 uses the fail-closed GitLab pipeline documented in
README.md:142:[docs/gitlab-release.md](docs/gitlab-release.md). Do not add or push a GitLab
README.md:143:remote until the Relux-owned hostname, namespace/project, project ID, release
README.md:144:account, accepted commit SHA, protected macOS runner, and protected credentials
README.md:145:are explicitly confirmed. `gitlab.wildberries.ru` is unrelated and prohibited
README.md:146:for this release.
README.md:147:
README.md:148:The existing GitHub tag workflow remains historical release automation. Both
README.md:149:paths require a real Developer ID signature; production mode in
README.md:150:`scripts/build-app.sh` refuses an ad-hoc fallback.
docs/gitlab-release.md:1:# GitLab macOS release runbook
docs/gitlab-release.md:2:
docs/gitlab-release.md:3:This runbook is the production path for Relux Proxy v0.2.0. It deliberately
docs/gitlab-release.md:4:fails closed until the Relux-owned GitLab project, accepted commit, protected
docs/gitlab-release.md:5:runner, and Apple credentials are all present.
docs/gitlab-release.md:6:
docs/gitlab-release.md:7:## Current external blocker
docs/gitlab-release.md:8:
docs/gitlab-release.md:9:As of 2026-07-28, the production release target is not confirmed:
docs/gitlab-release.md:10:
docs/gitlab-release.md:11:| Required identity | Current evidence |
docs/gitlab-release.md:12:| --- | --- |
docs/gitlab-release.md:13:| GitLab hostname | Not supplied. `gitlab.com` has no usable local API or SSH authorization. |
docs/gitlab-release.md:14:| Namespace/project | Not supplied. The only repository remote is `git@github.com:relux-works/relux-proxy.git`. |
docs/gitlab-release.md:15:| GitLab project ID | Not supplied. |
docs/gitlab-release.md:16:| Authorized release account | Not supplied. |
docs/gitlab-release.md:17:| Protected macOS release runner | Not discoverable without the target project. |
docs/gitlab-release.md:18:| Notarization credentials in GitLab | Not discoverable without the target project. |
docs/gitlab-release.md:19:
docs/gitlab-release.md:20:`gitlab.wildberries.ru` is explicitly unrelated and prohibited for every
docs/gitlab-release.md:21:Relux source push, tag, package, and Release operation. A credential happens to
docs/gitlab-release.md:22:exist for that host on one development machine; its presence is not
docs/gitlab-release.md:23:authorization to use it.
docs/gitlab-release.md:24:
docs/gitlab-release.md:25:Do not add a GitLab remote, push source, create a tag, or run a release until a
docs/gitlab-release.md:26:Relux owner replaces the unknown fields above with the exact hostname,
docs/gitlab-release.md:27:namespace/project, project ID, and authorized account in reviewed project
docs/gitlab-release.md:28:documentation.
docs/gitlab-release.md:29:
docs/gitlab-release.md:30:## One-time project and runner setup
docs/gitlab-release.md:31:
docs/gitlab-release.md:32:1. Confirm that the hostname and full namespace/project are owned by Relux.
docs/gitlab-release.md:33:   Confirm the project ID through the same project page or API and record the
docs/gitlab-release.md:34:   authorized operator.
docs/gitlab-release.md:35:2. Enable the project Package Registry and CI/CD.
docs/gitlab-release.md:36:3. Register a project-locked macOS runner using GitLab's supported user-mode
docs/gitlab-release.md:37:   `LaunchAgent` and shell executor. Give it both `macos` and `relux-release`
docs/gitlab-release.md:38:   tags, disable untagged jobs, mark it protected, and do not share it with
docs/gitlab-release.md:39:   untrusted projects. The runner needs Xcode, Swift, `glab`, `shellcheck`, and
docs/gitlab-release.md:40:   the standard macOS signing tools.
docs/gitlab-release.md:41:4. Protect the `v0.2.0` tag so only the release owner can create it.
docs/gitlab-release.md:42:5. Protect the `production` environment and require the project's chosen
docs/gitlab-release.md:43:   release approval.
docs/gitlab-release.md:44:6. Store the following project CI/CD variables. Secret values must be
docs/gitlab-release.md:45:   **Masked and hidden**, **Protected**, and have variable expansion disabled:
docs/gitlab-release.md:46:
docs/gitlab-release.md:47:   | Variable | Handling |
docs/gitlab-release.md:48:   | --- | --- |
docs/gitlab-release.md:49:   | `MACOS_CERT_P12_B64` | Base64-encoded Relux Works Developer ID Application certificate plus private key. Secret. |
docs/gitlab-release.md:50:   | `MACOS_CERT_PASSWORD` | Non-empty password for the PKCS#12 payload. Secret. |
docs/gitlab-release.md:51:   | `MACOS_KEYCHAIN_PASSWORD` | Temporary CI keychain password. Secret. |
docs/gitlab-release.md:52:   | `ASC_KEY_ID` | App Store Connect API key ID. Secret metadata. |
docs/gitlab-release.md:53:   | `ASC_ISSUER_ID` | App Store Connect issuer ID. Secret metadata. |
docs/gitlab-release.md:54:   | `ASC_KEY_P8_B64` | Base64-encoded App Store Connect API private key. Secret. |
docs/gitlab-release.md:55:
docs/gitlab-release.md:56:   Add these non-secret values as Protected variables:
docs/gitlab-release.md:57:
docs/gitlab-release.md:58:   | Variable | Required value |
docs/gitlab-release.md:59:   | --- | --- |
docs/gitlab-release.md:60:   | `RELUX_GITLAB_HOST` | Exact confirmed hostname, without a scheme. |
docs/gitlab-release.md:61:   | `RELUX_GITLAB_PROJECT_PATH` | Exact case-sensitive namespace/project path. |
docs/gitlab-release.md:62:   | `RELUX_ACCEPTED_RELEASE_SHA` | Full lowercase 40-character SHA accepted by review for v0.2.0. |
docs/gitlab-release.md:63:
docs/gitlab-release.md:64:GitLab documents that protected variables are limited to protected refs and
docs/gitlab-release.md:65:that sensitive values should be masked and hidden. Masking is only a log
docs/gitlab-release.md:66:defense, so the release runner and every pipeline change still require review:
docs/gitlab-release.md:67:<https://docs.gitlab.com/ci/variables/>.
docs/gitlab-release.md:68:
docs/gitlab-release.md:69:GitLab's supported macOS runner setup uses a user-mode `LaunchAgent`; code
docs/gitlab-release.md:70:signing requires that user session and keychain access:
docs/gitlab-release.md:71:<https://docs.gitlab.com/runner/install/osx/>.
docs/gitlab-release.md:72:
docs/gitlab-release.md:73:## Audited pipeline
docs/gitlab-release.md:74:
docs/gitlab-release.md:75:`.gitlab-ci.yml` has three gates:
docs/gitlab-release.md:76:
docs/gitlab-release.md:77:1. `validate` runs shell lint, Swift tests, a Swift build, and an ad-hoc-signed
docs/gitlab-release.md:78:   universal development bundle without production credentials.
docs/gitlab-release.md:79:2. `release_v0_2_0` runs only for the protected `v0.2.0` tag on the trusted
docs/gitlab-release.md:80:   `macos` + `relux-release` runner. It compares GitLab's actual host, project
docs/gitlab-release.md:81:   path, checked-out commit, and runner tags with the protected expected
docs/gitlab-release.md:82:   values. It imports the certificate into a disposable keychain; builds both
docs/gitlab-release.md:83:   `arm64` and `x86_64`; signs with the exact Relux Works Developer ID; submits
docs/gitlab-release.md:84:   the DMG with `notarytool`; requires `Accepted`; saves the notarization log;
docs/gitlab-release.md:85:   staples and validates the ticket; runs Gatekeeper; and produces checksums.
docs/gitlab-release.md:86:3. `verify_v0_2_0` downloads all three assets through the job-token-supported
docs/gitlab-release.md:87:   Releases API endpoint
docs/gitlab-release.md:88:   `CI_API_V4_URL/projects/$CI_PROJECT_ID/releases/$CI_COMMIT_TAG/downloads/<asset>`.
docs/gitlab-release.md:89:   Browser-style `/-/releases/` permanent links remain end-user URLs and are
docs/gitlab-release.md:90:   not used as authenticated CI API targets. The job verifies that the Release
docs/gitlab-release.md:91:   points to the accepted commit, compares the published checksum file with the
docs/gitlab-release.md:92:   CI-produced file, checks both hashes, and proves the stable and versioned
docs/gitlab-release.md:93:   DMGs are byte-identical.
docs/gitlab-release.md:94:
docs/gitlab-release.md:95:Production jobs are serialized through the
docs/gitlab-release.md:96:`relux-production-release` resource group. The certificate and API key are
docs/gitlab-release.md:97:decoded only under a `mktemp` directory; the temporary keychain and decoded
docs/gitlab-release.md:98:files are removed by an exit trap.
docs/gitlab-release.md:99:
docs/gitlab-release.md:100:The package upload uses the automatically scoped `CI_JOB_TOKEN`, following the
docs/gitlab-release.md:101:GitLab Generic Package Registry contract:
docs/gitlab-release.md:102:<https://docs.gitlab.com/user/packages/generic_packages/>.
docs/gitlab-release.md:103:The GitLab Release exposes the package objects as direct asset links:
docs/gitlab-release.md:104:<https://docs.gitlab.com/api/releases/links/>.
docs/gitlab-release.md:105:
docs/gitlab-release.md:106:## Source mirror and tag
docs/gitlab-release.md:107:
docs/gitlab-release.md:108:Only after the target table is explicit and authorization has been verified:
docs/gitlab-release.md:109:
docs/gitlab-release.md:110:```bash
docs/gitlab-release.md:111:git remote add relux-gitlab "git@<CONFIRMED_HOST>:<CONFIRMED_NAMESPACE>/relux-proxy.git"
docs/gitlab-release.md:112:git ls-remote relux-gitlab
docs/gitlab-release.md:113:git push --dry-run relux-gitlab "<ACCEPTED_SHA>:refs/heads/main"
docs/gitlab-release.md:114:git push relux-gitlab "<ACCEPTED_SHA>:refs/heads/main"
docs/gitlab-release.md:115:git tag -s v0.2.0 "<ACCEPTED_SHA>" -m "Relux Proxy v0.2.0"
docs/gitlab-release.md:116:git push relux-gitlab v0.2.0
docs/gitlab-release.md:117:```
docs/gitlab-release.md:118:
docs/gitlab-release.md:119:Stop if the remote default branch has unrelated history, the dry run names a
docs/gitlab-release.md:120:different project, the target values disagree, or the protected runner and
docs/gitlab-release.md:121:variables are absent. Do not force-push around such a mismatch.
docs/gitlab-release.md:122:
docs/gitlab-release.md:123:The protected tag pipeline publishes exactly:
docs/gitlab-release.md:124:
docs/gitlab-release.md:125:- `ReluxProxy-v0.2.0-universal.dmg`
docs/gitlab-release.md:126:- `ReluxProxy.dmg`
docs/gitlab-release.md:127:- `SHA256SUMS`
docs/gitlab-release.md:128:
docs/gitlab-release.md:129:The locally configured checkout remains the source of the accepted commit; no
docs/gitlab-release.md:130:uncommitted working-tree content can enter a tag pipeline.
docs/gitlab-release.md:131:
docs/gitlab-release.md:132:## Post-release evidence
docs/gitlab-release.md:133:
docs/gitlab-release.md:134:Keep the successful release and verification job artifacts for audit. They
docs/gitlab-release.md:135:include the source SHA, architectures, signing authority and team ID,
docs/gitlab-release.md:136:notarization response and log, Gatekeeper result, and published downloads.
docs/gitlab-release.md:137:
docs/gitlab-release.md:138:Verify the direct links in the confirmed project:
docs/gitlab-release.md:139:
docs/gitlab-release.md:140:```text
docs/gitlab-release.md:141:https://<CONFIRMED_HOST>/<CONFIRMED_NAMESPACE>/relux-proxy/-/releases/v0.2.0/downloads/ReluxProxy-v0.2.0-universal.dmg
docs/gitlab-release.md:142:https://<CONFIRMED_HOST>/<CONFIRMED_NAMESPACE>/relux-proxy/-/releases/v0.2.0/downloads/ReluxProxy.dmg
docs/gitlab-release.md:143:https://<CONFIRMED_HOST>/<CONFIRMED_NAMESPACE>/relux-proxy/-/releases/v0.2.0/downloads/SHA256SUMS
docs/gitlab-release.md:144:```
docs/gitlab-release.md:145:
docs/gitlab-release.md:146:An unsigned, ad-hoc-signed, unstapled, Gatekeeper-rejected, wrong-host, or
docs/gitlab-release.md:147:wrong-commit artifact is a failed candidate and must never be described as the
docs/gitlab-release.md:148:production release.
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:1:# Legacy SOCKS multi-tunnel contract
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:2:
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:3:**Contract version:** 1.1
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:4:**Status:** shipped product behavior in Relux Proxy v0.2.0; production GitLab
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:5:publication remains gated on the external release prerequisites
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:6:**Applies to:** the system-OpenSSH SOCKS product only; it does not define the
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:7:future system VPN.
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:8:
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:9:This is the source of truth for the v0.2.0 extension of the macOS menu-bar SOCKS
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:10:app from one tunnel to independently controlled, concurrent tunnels. The
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:11:behavior described here is implemented and accepted in the v0.2.0 source
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:12:candidate; it is no longer planned-only work. Production distribution is a
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:13:separate evidence gate: as of 2026-07-28, no GitLab release is claimed because
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:14:the Relux-owned GitLab project and authorization have not been supplied. The
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:15:future VPN remains governed by the other documents in this directory.
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:16:
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:17:The key words **MUST**, **SHOULD**, and **MAY** are normative.
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:18:
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:19:## 1. Product boundary
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:20:
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:21:A user MUST be able to save two or more SSH SOCKS profiles, choose each local
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:22:SOCKS port explicitly, and run profiles concurrently when their ports differ.
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:23:Each profile is independently started, stopped, diagnosed, edited, and removed.
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:24:
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:25:Every tunnel MUST use the macOS system executable /usr/bin/ssh. Relux Proxy
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:26:does not parse, rewrite, import, or reimplement OpenSSH configuration semantics.
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:27:The app remains a manual, local-only SOCKS endpoint: users configure a
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:28:SOCKS-aware client themselves.
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:29:
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:30:The current one-tunnel implementation and its sshHost, sshAccount, and
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:31:localPort preferences are migration inputs, not a second live configuration
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:32:model after migration.
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:33:
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:34:## 2. Persisted profile model
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:35:
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:36:### 2.1 Versioned store
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:37:
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:38:The app MUST store the profile collection as one JSON Data value in the standard
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:39:app UserDefaults suite under the key legacySocksProfiles.v1. The payload has
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:40:this exact v1 shape:
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:41:
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:42:    {
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:43:      "schemaVersion": 1,
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:44:      "profiles": [
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:45:        {
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:46:          "id": "4CA3B171-4C88-4B84-A33C-340B9F28DFF8",
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:47:          "host": "work-bastion",
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:48:          "account": "",
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:49:          "localPort": 1080
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:50:        }
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:51:      ]
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:52:    }
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:53:
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:54:profiles preserves its array order. Every profile field is required:
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:55:
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:56:| Field | Requirement |
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:57:| --- | --- |
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:58:| id | A unique, stable UUID string. It is the runtime and UI identity; host or port changes do not create a second identity. |
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:59:| host | The trimmed SSH destination token. It is non-empty, contains no whitespace, and does not begin with -. Its spelling and case are otherwise preserved. |
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:60:| account | The trimmed optional SSH account. An empty string is valid. A non-empty value contains neither whitespace nor @. |
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:61:| localPort | An integer in 1...65535, unique across the entire persisted profile list. |
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:62:
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:63:The list is validated as a whole before a write. A failed add or edit MUST leave
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:64:the previous store intact; a duplicate port is rejected even when neither profile
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:65:is running. The product MUST NOT choose an ephemeral port, silently substitute
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:66:another port, or derive a port from the host.
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:67:
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:68:Runtime state is deliberately absent from this payload. The app MUST NOT persist
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:69:connection state, process IDs, port reservations, diagnostics, or a reconnect
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:70:intent.
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:71:
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:72:If the payload has an unknown newer schemaVersion, invalid required fields, or
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:73:invalid JSON, the app MUST preserve it without overwriting it, report a profile
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:74:persistence error, and refuse to start affected profiles. It MUST NOT fall back
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:75:to the legacy keys or silently create replacement profiles. A separately
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:76:confirmed reset MAY replace the invalid payload with an empty v1 store.
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:77:
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:78:### 2.2 One-time migration from the single-tunnel preferences
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:79:
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:80:The completion marker is the Boolean legacySocksProfiles.migration.v1.complete.
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:81:Migration is idempotent and follows this order on startup:
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:82:
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:83:1. If a valid v1 payload already exists, load it. If the marker is absent, set
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:84:   the marker; do not create another profile.
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:85:2. If no v1 payload exists and the marker is absent, read legacy sshHost,
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:86:   sshAccount, and localPort. A missing value uses the current single-tunnel
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:87:   default for that field: relux, administrator, or 1080.
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:88:3. Trim and validate the resulting tuple with the v1 rules. When valid, write a
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:89:   v1 payload containing exactly one newly generated profile. When invalid or
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:90:   stored with an incompatible type, write an empty v1 payload and surface a
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:91:   bounded migration diagnostic rather than creating a partial profile.
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:92:4. Persist the v1 payload before setting the completion marker. A restart after
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:93:   a crash between those writes follows step 1, so it cannot duplicate a
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:94:   profile.
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:95:5. Once the marker is true, legacy keys are never read for migration again.
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:96:   This prevents deleted profiles from being resurrected on a later launch.
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:97:
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:98:If the marker is already true but the v1 payload is absent, initialize an empty
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:99:v1 payload; do not revisit legacy keys.
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:100:
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:101:Migration MUST leave sshHost, sshAccount, and localPort untouched for rollback
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:102:compatibility with the old application. The new implementation never writes
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:103:those legacy keys. An intentionally empty v1 profile list is valid and is not
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:104:repopulated from legacy preferences.
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:105:
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:106:## 3. SSH destination and OpenSSH semantics
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:107:
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:108:### 3.1 Destination input
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:109:
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:110:host is one opaque destination argument for /usr/bin/ssh; it is not resolved or
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:111:classified by Relux Proxy. The same field therefore accepts all of the following
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:112:when OpenSSH accepts them:
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:113:
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:114:| User input | Required handling |
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:115:| --- | --- |
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:116:| 203.0.113.10 or an IPv6 literal | Pass it as the destination token; do not turn it into an alias or perform app-side DNS resolution. |
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:117:| bastion.example.net | Pass it as the destination token; system OpenSSH resolves it and applies matching configuration. |
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:118:| work-bastion where Host work-bastion exists in ~/.ssh/config | Pass the alias unchanged. The system client evaluates that Host stanza and applicable Include or wildcard rules. |
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:119:
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:120:The runner MUST use process argument APIs, never a shell command string. It
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:121:MUST NOT split a destination, synthesize an SSH configuration file, or inspect
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:122:~/.ssh/config to choose a key, port, jump host, or resolved hostname.
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:123:
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:124:### 3.2 Account precedence
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:125:
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:126:The normalized account field has exactly two modes:
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:127:
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:128:| Account value | Required invocation behavior | Resulting User precedence |
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:129:| --- | --- | --- |
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:130:| Empty | Omit -l and pass the bare host destination. Do not construct @host. | User from a matching OpenSSH configuration stanza applies; if none applies, OpenSSH uses its normal system/default login-user behavior. |
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:131:| Non-empty | Pass -l account as a separate command-line option and pass the bare host destination. | The command-line account explicitly overrides configured User; no app-side configuration is changed. |
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:132:
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:133:For example, with Host work-bastion, User deploy, HostName 10.0.0.8, and Port
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:134:2202 in ~/.ssh/config, an empty account connects as OpenSSH's configured deploy
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:135:user. An account of ops connects as ops; the alias still supplies its hostname
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:136:and port.
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:137:
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:138:### 3.3 What the system client owns
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:139:
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:140:Each launch retains the current SOCKS command behavior:
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:141:
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:142:    /usr/bin/ssh -N -C -D 127.0.0.1:<localPort>
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:143:      -o ExitOnForwardFailure=yes
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:144:      -o ServerAliveInterval=30
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:145:      -o ServerAliveCountMax=3
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:146:      -o ConnectTimeout=15
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:147:      -o BatchMode=yes
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:148:      -o LogLevel=ERROR
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:149:      [-l <account>]
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:150:      <host>
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:151:
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:152:Apart from those fixed operational options and the optional account override,
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:153:the installed OpenSSH client retains normal precedence and configuration
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:154:behavior. In particular, a matching alias MAY provide HostName, Port,
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:155:IdentityFile, ProxyJump, and related standard options. Relux Proxy MUST NOT add
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:156:command-line overrides for those settings.
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:157:
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:158:Relux Proxy MUST NOT pass StrictHostKeyChecking, UserKnownHostsFile,
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:159:GlobalKnownHostsFile, or another host-key-policy override. Existing known_hosts
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:160:files, system defaults, and the user's OpenSSH configuration remain
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:161:authoritative. BatchMode=yes preserves the current non-interactive product
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:162:behavior; the app does not provide a host-key prompt, approve a key, or weaken
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:163:host-key verification.
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:164:
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:165:## 4. Local port ownership
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:166:
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:167:Every dynamic forward MUST bind exactly 127.0.0.1:<localPort>. IPv6 loopback,
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:168:localhost, all interfaces, LAN interfaces, 0.0.0.0, and every non-loopback
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:169:address are prohibited. The app MUST NOT enable a public proxy through
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:170:GatewayPorts or an equivalent option.
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:171:
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:172:The profile registry owns the requested port while that profile is starting,
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:173:connected, or stopping. A start MUST reserve the profile and port in the
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:174:in-process registry before spawning SSH, so duplicate start requests cannot
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:175:create two children for the same profile. The implementation MAY probe whether
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:176:127.0.0.1:<localPort> is available before launch, but that probe is advisory:
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:177:the SSH process with ExitOnForwardFailure=yes is authoritative because a race
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:178:with another local process remains possible.
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:179:
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:180:If a port is occupied by another profile, another application, or a surviving
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:181:child from an unclean prior exit, only the requested profile enters failed. The
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:182:app MUST NOT stop, reconfigure, replace, or kill a process it did not spawn in
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:183:the current run. Its diagnostic identifies the local endpoint and asks the user
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:184:to stop the owner or choose a different port. No other tunnel is affected.
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:185:
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:186:## 5. Concurrent lifecycle contract
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:187:
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:188:### 5.1 Independent runtime instances
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:189:
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:190:The runtime registry is keyed by profile UUID. Each entry owns one immutable
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:191:configuration snapshot, one SSH child, one diagnostic buffer, and one lifecycle
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:192:generation token. Completion, readiness, stderr, and termination callbacks MUST
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:193:check that token before changing state; a late callback from an old child cannot
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:194:mark a restarted or removed profile as connected or failed.
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:195:
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:196:Each profile has these visible states:
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:197:
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:198:    disconnected -> connecting -> connected -> disconnecting -> disconnected
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:199:                        |             |
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:200:                        +-----------> failed -> connecting
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:201:
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:202:An explicit stop during connecting is cancellation and follows the
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:203:disconnecting -> disconnected path. An unexpected child exit, forward failure,
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:204:readiness timeout, invalid configuration, or unavailable port moves only that
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:205:profile to failed. A profile remains failed until the user explicitly starts it
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:206:again; there is no automatic retry or reconnect.
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:207:
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:208:connected requires both that the current-generation SSH child is still running
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:209:and that the expected 127.0.0.1:<localPort> endpoint accepts a connection. If
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:210:the child exits, its termination result wins over a racing readiness success.
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:211:The readiness timeout is 20 seconds, preserving the current 100 probes at
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:212:200 ms contract.
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:213:
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:214:Starting, stopping, or failing one profile MUST NOT change another profile's
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:215:state, child process, diagnostic buffer, or configuration. Two or more profiles
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:216:with unique ports may therefore be connected at the same time.
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:217:
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:218:### 5.2 Editing and removal
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:219:
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:220:An active profile (connecting, connected, or disconnecting) is immutable. The
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:221:UI and model MUST reject host, account, and port edits while it is active; they
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:222:MUST NOT silently retarget, restart, or mutate a running child.
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:223:
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:224:Removal is also rejected while a profile is active. The user must explicitly
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:225:stop it and wait until it is disconnected or a fully cleaned-up failed state
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:226:before removal. This prevents an orphaned SSH child from losing its owning
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:227:profile and accidental port reuse while an old process is exiting.
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:228:
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:229:For an inactive profile, an edit validates the complete collection (including
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:230:port uniqueness) and persists atomically. A subsequent start uses the saved
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:231:snapshot. Removing an inactive profile removes only its persistent record and
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:232:runtime entry; it does not alter any other profile.
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:233:
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:234:### 5.3 Stop, quit, and restart
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:235:
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:236:An explicit stop sends a termination request to that profile's current child
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:237:and enters disconnecting. After two seconds, the app MAY escalate to a forced
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:238:termination only for that same child handle if it is still running. It MUST
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:239:observe termination before releasing the runtime reservation and report
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:240:disconnected for a user-requested stop.
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:241:
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:242:On normal app quit, the app applies the same stop procedure to every child it
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:243:owns. It MUST never discover and kill processes merely by port, host, or command
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:244:line. A child that survived an unclean app crash is not considered owned after a
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:245:restart; a later port conflict is handled as the isolated failure in section 4.
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:246:
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:247:After every launch, all persisted profiles begin disconnected, with no
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:248:persisted diagnostic or connection intent. The app MUST NOT automatically
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:249:reconnect, restore active tunnels, or modify another process to recreate the
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:250:pre-quit state.
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:251:
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:252:## 6. Diagnostics and failure isolation
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:253:
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:254:Diagnostics are per profile and runtime-only. Starting a profile clears its
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:255:prior diagnostic. Stderr from that profile's SSH child and generated launch or
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:256:readiness errors append only to its buffer. After each append, the product MUST
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:257:retain only the trailing 4,000 user-visible characters, matching the current
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:258:single-tunnel bound.
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:259:
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:260:On an unexpected exit, the failure summary is the last non-empty diagnostic line
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:261:when available, otherwise the SSH exit status. A user-requested stop clears the
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:262:diagnostic after termination. Diagnostics MUST NOT be shared across profiles,
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:263:written as a persistent profile field, uploaded, or used as telemetry.
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:264:
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:265:## 7. Explicit non-goals
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:266:
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:267:This contract does **not** authorize:
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:268:
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:269:- automatic reconnect, retry backoff, or restoration of tunnels after launch;
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:270:- mutation of macOS system proxy, PAC, browser, DNS, or routing settings;
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:271:- VPN behavior, NETunnelProviderManager, a Network Extension, packet-tunnel
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:272:  entitlements, or system-wide traffic capture/routing;
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:273:- editing, generating, importing, or rewriting ~/.ssh/config, known_hosts, SSH
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:274:  keys, passphrases, or identities;
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:275:- an embedded SSH client or replacement for /usr/bin/ssh;
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:276:- public, LAN, IPv6 local-listener, or non-loopback SOCKS exposure;
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:277:- automatic local-port allocation or a shared port for multiple profiles.
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:278:
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:279:Future VPN work has separate product, architecture, security, entitlement, and
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:280:release gates. It does not implicitly inherit this legacy SOCKS persistence or
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:281:OpenSSH-alias contract.
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:282:
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:283:## 8. Required implementation evidence
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:284:
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:285:An implementation of this contract MUST demonstrate, with automated tests or
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:286:deterministic fakes where a live SSH server is unnecessary:
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:287:
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:288:1. migration of valid legacy values into one v1 profile, idempotence across a
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:289:   restart, invalid-input handling, and non-resurrection after deleting all
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:290:   profiles;
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:291:2. exact account behavior for an IP, DNS hostname, and Host alias: empty account
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:292:   omits -l, while a non-empty account passes -l and overrides only User;
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:293:3. rejection of duplicate, invalid, and externally occupied local ports without
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:294:   interrupting an already connected profile;
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:295:4. independent concurrent start, failure, stop, and diagnostic buffers for at
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:296:   least two unique ports, including stale-callback protection;
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:297:5. rejection of active-profile edits and removal, orderly normal-quit child
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:298:   termination, and a restart with every profile disconnected and no auto-retry.
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:299:
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:300:## 9. Version history
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:301:
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:302:| Version | Date | Change |
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:303:| --- | --- | --- |
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:304:| 1.1 | 2026-07-28 | Recorded the implemented v0.2.0 multi-profile behavior as shipped product scope while keeping GitLab publication explicitly gated. |
.spec/legacy-socks-multi-tunnel.md:305:| 1.0 | 2026-07-28 | Initial contract for the planned multi-profile legacy SOCKS extension. |
release-notes/v0.2.0.md:1:# Relux Proxy v0.2.0
release-notes/v0.2.0.md:2:
release-notes/v0.2.0.md:3:Relux Proxy v0.2.0 adds saved, independently controlled SSH SOCKS profiles to
release-notes/v0.2.0.md:4:the native macOS menu-bar app.
release-notes/v0.2.0.md:5:
release-notes/v0.2.0.md:6:## Highlights
release-notes/v0.2.0.md:7:
release-notes/v0.2.0.md:8:- Save multiple profiles with stable identity and a unique local port.
release-notes/v0.2.0.md:9:- Connect multiple profiles at once as concurrent local SOCKS endpoints on
release-notes/v0.2.0.md:10:  `127.0.0.1`.
release-notes/v0.2.0.md:11:- Use an IP address, DNS hostname, or SSH config alias without Relux Proxy
release-notes/v0.2.0.md:12:  parsing or rewriting `~/.ssh/config`.
release-notes/v0.2.0.md:13:- Leave Account empty to preserve the `User` selected by OpenSSH for a matching
release-notes/v0.2.0.md:14:  SSH config alias, or set Account to pass an explicit `-l` user override.
release-notes/v0.2.0.md:15:- Start, stop, diagnose, edit, and remove profiles independently. A failed or
release-notes/v0.2.0.md:16:  occupied port affects only that profile.
release-notes/v0.2.0.md:17:- Migrate the previous single-tunnel preferences once while retaining the old
release-notes/v0.2.0.md:18:  keys for rollback compatibility.
release-notes/v0.2.0.md:19:
release-notes/v0.2.0.md:20:Relux Proxy continues to launch the system `/usr/bin/ssh` client. Each endpoint
release-notes/v0.2.0.md:21:is an application SOCKS5 proxy for TCP traffic, not a system VPN. SOCKS-aware
release-notes/v0.2.0.md:22:clients should enable SOCKS5h or proxy DNS when the remote SSH host must resolve
release-notes/v0.2.0.md:23:private names.
release-notes/v0.2.0.md:24:
release-notes/v0.2.0.md:25:## Assets
release-notes/v0.2.0.md:26:
release-notes/v0.2.0.md:27:- `ReluxProxy-v0.2.0-universal.dmg` — versioned Apple silicon and Intel build.
release-notes/v0.2.0.md:28:- `ReluxProxy.dmg` — stable-name copy of the same notarized DMG.
release-notes/v0.2.0.md:29:- `SHA256SUMS` — SHA-256 hashes for both DMG names.
release-notes/v0.2.0.md:30:
release-notes/v0.2.0.md:31:The production GitLab pipeline publishes these assets only after verifying the
release-notes/v0.2.0.md:32:accepted source commit, Relux GitLab project identity, Developer ID signature,
release-notes/v0.2.0.md:33:universal architectures, Apple notarization ticket, stapling, Gatekeeper
release-notes/v0.2.0.md:34:assessment, and checksums.

