1:# GitLab macOS release runbook
2:
3:This runbook is the production path for Relux Proxy v0.2.0. It deliberately
4:fails closed until the Relux-owned GitLab project, accepted commit, protected
5:runner, and Apple credentials are all present.
6:
7:## Current external blocker
8:
9:As of 2026-07-28, the production release target is not confirmed:
10:
11:| Required identity | Current evidence |
12:| --- | --- |
13:| GitLab hostname | Not supplied. `gitlab.com` has no usable local API or SSH authorization. |
14:| Namespace/project | Not supplied. The only repository remote is `git@github.com:relux-works/relux-proxy.git`. |
15:| GitLab project ID | Not supplied. |
16:| Authorized release account | Not supplied. |
17:| Protected macOS release runner | Not discoverable without the target project. |
18:| Notarization credentials in GitLab | Not discoverable without the target project. |
19:
20:`gitlab.wildberries.ru` is explicitly unrelated and prohibited for every
21:Relux source push, tag, package, and Release operation. A credential happens to
22:exist for that host on one development machine; its presence is not
23:authorization to use it.
24:
25:Do not add a GitLab remote, push source, create a tag, or run a release until a
26:Relux owner replaces the unknown fields above with the exact hostname,
27:namespace/project, project ID, and authorized account in reviewed project
28:documentation.
29:
30:## One-time project and runner setup
31:
32:1. Confirm that the hostname and full namespace/project are owned by Relux.
33:   Confirm the project ID through the same project page or API and record the
34:   authorized operator.
35:2. Enable the project Package Registry and CI/CD.
36:3. Register a project-locked macOS runner using GitLab's supported user-mode
37:   `LaunchAgent` and shell executor. Give it both `macos` and `relux-release`
38:   tags, disable untagged jobs, mark it protected, and do not share it with
39:   untrusted projects. The runner needs Xcode, Swift, `glab`, `shellcheck`, and
40:   the standard macOS signing tools.
41:4. Protect the `v0.2.0` tag so only the release owner can create it.
42:5. Protect the `production` environment and require the project's chosen
43:   release approval.
44:6. Store the following project CI/CD variables. Secret values must be
45:   **Masked and hidden**, **Protected**, and have variable expansion disabled:
46:
47:   | Variable | Handling |
48:   | --- | --- |
49:   | `MACOS_CERT_P12_B64` | Base64-encoded Relux Works Developer ID Application certificate plus private key. Secret. |
50:   | `MACOS_CERT_PASSWORD` | Non-empty password for the PKCS#12 payload. Secret. |
51:   | `MACOS_KEYCHAIN_PASSWORD` | Temporary CI keychain password. Secret. |
52:   | `ASC_KEY_ID` | App Store Connect API key ID. Secret metadata. |
53:   | `ASC_ISSUER_ID` | App Store Connect issuer ID. Secret metadata. |
54:   | `ASC_KEY_P8_B64` | Base64-encoded App Store Connect API private key. Secret. |
55:
56:   Add these non-secret values as Protected variables:
57:
58:   | Variable | Required value |
59:   | --- | --- |
60:   | `RELUX_GITLAB_HOST` | Exact confirmed hostname, without a scheme. |
61:   | `RELUX_GITLAB_PROJECT_PATH` | Exact case-sensitive namespace/project path. |
62:   | `RELUX_ACCEPTED_RELEASE_SHA` | Full lowercase 40-character SHA accepted by review for v0.2.0. |
63:
64:GitLab documents that protected variables are limited to protected refs and
65:that sensitive values should be masked and hidden. Masking is only a log
66:defense, so the release runner and every pipeline change still require review:
67:<https://docs.gitlab.com/ci/variables/>.
68:
69:GitLab's supported macOS runner setup uses a user-mode `LaunchAgent`; code
70:signing requires that user session and keychain access:
71:<https://docs.gitlab.com/runner/install/osx/>.
72:
73:## Audited pipeline
74:
75:`.gitlab-ci.yml` has three gates:
76:
77:1. `validate` runs shell lint, Swift tests, a Swift build, and an ad-hoc-signed
78:   universal development bundle without production credentials.
79:2. `release_v0_2_0` runs only for the protected `v0.2.0` tag on the trusted
80:   `macos` + `relux-release` runner. It compares GitLab's actual host, project
81:   path, checked-out commit, and runner tags with the protected expected
82:   values. It imports the certificate into a disposable keychain; builds both
83:   `arm64` and `x86_64`; signs with the exact Relux Works Developer ID; submits
84:   the DMG with `notarytool`; requires `Accepted`; saves the notarization log;
85:   staples and validates the ticket; runs Gatekeeper; and produces checksums.
86:3. `verify_v0_2_0` downloads all three assets through the job-token-supported
87:   Releases API endpoint
88:   `CI_API_V4_URL/projects/$CI_PROJECT_ID/releases/$CI_COMMIT_TAG/downloads/<asset>`.
89:   Browser-style `/-/releases/` permanent links remain end-user URLs and are
90:   not used as authenticated CI API targets. The job verifies that the Release
91:   points to the accepted commit, compares the published checksum file with the
92:   CI-produced file, checks both hashes, and proves the stable and versioned
93:   DMGs are byte-identical.
94:
95:Production jobs are serialized through the
96:`relux-production-release` resource group. The certificate and API key are
97:decoded only under a `mktemp` directory; the temporary keychain and decoded
98:files are removed by an exit trap.
99:
100:The package upload uses the automatically scoped `CI_JOB_TOKEN`, following the
101:GitLab Generic Package Registry contract:
102:<https://docs.gitlab.com/user/packages/generic_packages/>.
103:The GitLab Release exposes the package objects as direct asset links:
104:<https://docs.gitlab.com/api/releases/links/>.
105:
106:## Source mirror and tag
107:
108:Only after the target table is explicit and authorization has been verified:
109:
110:```bash
111:git remote add relux-gitlab "git@<CONFIRMED_HOST>:<CONFIRMED_NAMESPACE>/relux-proxy.git"
112:git ls-remote relux-gitlab
113:git push --dry-run relux-gitlab "<ACCEPTED_SHA>:refs/heads/main"
114:git push relux-gitlab "<ACCEPTED_SHA>:refs/heads/main"
115:git tag -s v0.2.0 "<ACCEPTED_SHA>" -m "Relux Proxy v0.2.0"
116:git push relux-gitlab v0.2.0
117:```
118:
119:Stop if the remote default branch has unrelated history, the dry run names a
120:different project, the target values disagree, or the protected runner and
121:variables are absent. Do not force-push around such a mismatch.
122:
123:The protected tag pipeline publishes exactly:
124:
125:- `ReluxProxy-v0.2.0-universal.dmg`
126:- `ReluxProxy.dmg`
